 "article": {
  "title": "The Long Journey Home: How Birds Find Their Way",
  "topic": "自然科普·野生动物（候鸟迁徙）",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 326,
  "paras": [
   "Every autumn, a quiet adventure begins in the skies above us. Millions of birds leave the places where they raised their young and fly south for the winter. Some, like the tiny warbler, travel more than three thousand kilometres. Others, such as geese, form long lines and call to each other as they cross whole continents. To us the journey may look simple, but it is one of the most impressive feats in the natural world.",
   "How do these travellers find their way? Scientists have learned that birds use several tools at once. During the day they observe the position of the sun; at night they read the stars. Many species can also sense the Earth's magnetic field, a kind of inner compass. Older birds seem to remember landmarks such as a river, a coastline or a mountain, so that the cycle repeats year after year with surprising accuracy.",
   "The trip is far from easy. Birds must cross oceans and deserts, face storms and hunters, and find enough food to keep going. Some lose almost half their weight before they reach their destination, and not all of them survive. Yet the young of many species set off alone, without ever having made the journey before, guided by instinct rather than memory. Their bodies change as well: they store extra fat in advance, exactly like a traveller packing for a long trip.",
   "Why should we care? Because migrating birds connect distant places. They carry seeds, control insects, and act as living signs of the health of our environment. When their numbers fall, it warns us that something is wrong along the route. Scientists now follow them with tiny tags and satellites, yet many questions remain. As the writer Rachel Carson reminded us, those who open their eyes to nature are never truly alone. Watching a flock of geese cross the evening sky, we can still feel the old wonder."
  ],
  "zh": "每年秋天，数百万只候鸟离开繁殖地南飞越冬：小巧的莺要飞三千多公里，大雁则排成长队、彼此呼应着横越整片大陆。科学家发现鸟类同时使用多种导航工具——白天看太阳，夜里读星星，许多种类还能感知地球磁场，像自带一枚罗盘，年长的鸟还会记住河流、海岸、山峰等地标，让这场循环年复一年地精准重复。旅途并不轻松：要飞越海洋与沙漠、抵御风暴与捕猎，抵达目的地前体重可能减掉近一半，不少鸟死在途中；可许多幼鸟却能在从未走过这条路的情况下独自启程，靠的是本能而非记忆，它们的身体还会提前储存脂肪，像出远门前打点行囊的旅人。为什么要在意？因为候鸟把遥远的地方连在一起：它们传播种子、控制昆虫，是环境健康的活标志；数量下降就是在提醒我们沿途出了问题。科学家如今用微型标签和卫星追踪它们，但谜团仍然很多——正如蕾切尔·卡森所提醒的，愿意睁眼看自然的人永远不会真正孤单。",
